Hi, thought I would share this.

If you care about the first impression your kernel makes :-) , or your
device has no serial ports, you might want to have the decompressor
output its messages through LCD, like on a VGA. In this case, use the
attached patch. The code is borrowed from the Acorn RiscPC
decompressor (rmk) and the other part changes the 8x8 font to a 8x16
one (you can dump half of the patch if 8x8 is ok).

In addition to the patch you will need to make your bootloader prepare
the LCDC and add a special ATAG to the boot tags. The tag contains
seven 32-bit numbers following the tag header:
framebuffer address in memory (can be any free piece of memory),
number of text columns (i.e. LCD width in px / 8)
number of text rows (i.e. LCD height in px / 16)
current cursor position column,
current cursor position row,
character width in px (8),
character height in px (16).

On Palm Tungsten E (320x320 LCD) the bootloader part looks somethine
like the following (fb_base is the framebuffer address and fb_end is
fb_base + 102910), the whole sequence can be figured out looking at
the Linux LCD driver.

    omap_writew(0x0000, 0xfffedb00);
    omap_writew(fb_base >> 16, 0xfffedb04);
    omap_writew(fb_base & 0xffff, 0xfffedb02);
    omap_writew(fb_end >> 16, 0xfffedb08);
    omap_writew(fb_end & 0xffff, 0xfffedb06);
    omap_writel(0x00000000, 0xfffec014);
    omap_writel(0xff300000, 0xfffec00c);
    omap_writel(0x1b070d3f, 0xfffec004);
    omap_writel(0x0708013f, 0xfffec008);
    omap_writel(0x03300004, 0xfffec00c);
    omap_writel(0xfe810c81, 0xfffec000);

Greetings

